Step-by-step approach to debug a failing API using logs

Let’s assume an API is failing (e.g., 500 / 503 / timeout).

1. 🔍 Start with the API request details

First, reproduce the issue using a tool like Postman or logs from the client side.

Capture:

Endpoint (/login, /payment)
HTTP method (GET/POST)
Request payload
Headers (auth token, content-type)
Timestamp

👉 This timestamp is critical for log tracing.

2. 🌐 Check API gateway / web server logs

If your system uses Nginx / Apache / load balancer:

Example logs:
GET /api/payment → 503 upstream failed

Look for:

4xx / 5xx errors
upstream timeout
connection refused

👉 This tells you if the request even reached the backend.

3. 🧾 Check application logs (most important step)

Go to application logs:

Common RHEL paths:

/var/log/app.log
/opt/app/logs/application.log

Search using timestamp:

grep "2026-05-12 10:15" app.log
What to look for:
Exceptions (NullPointerException, RuntimeException)
Validation errors
Authentication failures
Business logic failures

Example:

ERROR PaymentService: Null pointer exception at processPayment()
4. 🔌 Trace request flow in logs

Good systems log request IDs.

Example:

RequestID: abc123 - Start processing
RequestID: abc123 - Calling payment service
RequestID: abc123 - ERROR timeout

👉 This helps you follow one request across multiple services.

5. 🗄️ Check dependency logs (DB / external APIs)

If API depends on:

Database
Redis/cache
Third-party services

Check their logs:

Database example:
ERROR: connection timeout
ERROR: too many connections

👉 If DB fails, API often returns 500/503.

6. 🖥️ Check system logs (OS-level issues)

On RHEL systems:

/var/log/messages

Look for:

Out of memory (OOM killer)
Disk full
Service restart/crash

Example:

Out of memory: Killed process java
7. 📊 Check performance metrics (optional but powerful)

If logs are not enough, check:

CPU usage
Memory usage
Thread pool saturation

Commands:

top
free -m
df -h

👉 Overload often causes 503/timeout.


500 – Internal Server Error

This is a general “something broke on the server” error.

Common reasons:

Bug in server-side code (PHP, Node.js, Python, etc.)
Unhandled exception or crash in application logic
Misconfigured server settings (e.g., .htaccess, Nginx config)
Database connection failure or query error
File permission issues preventing execution or access
501 – Not Implemented

Server does not support the requested functionality.

Common reasons:

HTTP method not supported (e.g., PATCH/DELETE not enabled)
Feature not implemented in backend software
Outdated server software lacking support for modern protocols
API endpoint exists but is not developed yet
Reverse proxy misconfiguration blocking method handling
502 – Bad Gateway

One server received an invalid response from another server.

Common reasons:

Upstream server is down or crashed
Nginx/Apache acting as proxy gets invalid response
Network issues between load balancer and backend
Firewall blocking communication between servers
Timeout from backend service causing malformed response
503 – Service Unavailable

Server is temporarily overloaded or under maintenance.

Common reasons:

Server overloaded with too many requests (traffic spike)
Scheduled maintenance or deployment in progress
Backend services (DB/API) temporarily down
Resource exhaustion (CPU/RAM limits reached)
Auto-scaling failure in cloud infrastructure
504 – Gateway Timeout

A server didn’t get a response in time from another server.

Common reasons:

Slow database query taking too long
Backend API response delay or hang
Network latency between proxy and upstream server
Server overload causing delayed processing
Infinite loop or inefficient code in backend
505 – HTTP Version Not Supported

Server does not support the HTTP protocol version used.

Common reasons:

Client uses outdated or experimental HTTP version
Server configured only for specific HTTP versions (e.g., HTTP/1.1 only)
Proxy or load balancer stripping protocol info
Misconfigured TLS/HTTPS setup
Legacy server software without modern protocol support
Quick Summary
500 = Server broke unexpectedly
501 = Feature not supported
502 = Bad response from upstream server
503 = Server unavailable (overload/maintenance)
504 = Timeout waiting for upstream response
505 = HTTP version not supported


Step-by-step debugging workflow (works for all 5xx errors)
1. Start with the exact error context

Before guessing anything, capture:

URL that failed
Time of request
HTTP status code (500/502/503/504 etc.)
Request headers + payload (if API)
Correlation/request ID (if your system has it)

👉 If you don’t have request IDs, add them immediately in production.

2. Check reverse proxy logs (first stop)

If you're using Nginx / Apache / load balancer, check:

