When an API returns a 500 Internal Server Error, logs are your main way to find the real cause. A 500 is just the symptom—the logs reveal the actual failure.

Here’s exactly what you should look for, in a structured way.

🔍 1. First: locate the exact request

Start by finding the log entry for the failing request.

Look for:

timestamp of the failure
endpoint (/api/users, /login, etc.)
HTTP method (GET/POST/etc.)
request ID / correlation ID (if available)
user/session ID (if logged)

👉 This helps you isolate one request among millions.

💥 2. Check for stack traces (MOST IMPORTANT)

A 500 almost always comes with a stack trace in application logs.

Look for:

Exception
Traceback (Python)
Error:
UnhandledPromiseRejection (Node.js)
NullPointerException (Java)
What you want to extract:
exact error type
file + line number
function where it crashed

Example clues:

TypeError: Cannot read property 'id' of undefined
at UserService.getUser (user.service.js:45)

👉 This usually directly points to the bug.

🧠 3. Look at input data (request payload)

A huge percentage of 500 errors come from bad or unexpected input.

Check:

request body (JSON/form data)
query parameters
headers (Authorization, Content-Type)

Look for:

missing required fields
wrong data types (string vs number)
null/empty values
malformed JSON

👉 Example issue:

"userId": null → backend assumes it's a number → crash
🗄️ 4. Check database-related logs

Many 500 errors are DB failures.

Look for:

SQL errors
connection failures
timeout errors
deadlocks

Common log patterns:

ER_LOCK_DEADLOCK
connection refused
timeout expired
duplicate key violation

Also check:

slow queries
missing indexes
connection pool exhaustion
🌐 5. Look for downstream service/API failures

If your API calls other services:

Check logs for:

HTTP 4xx/5xx from external APIs
timeouts
DNS failures
connection refused

Example:

Failed to fetch payment service: timeout after 10s

👉 This often causes cascading 500 errors.

⏱️ 6. Look for timeouts

A 500 can happen when a request takes too long and gets aborted.

Check:

request duration
middleware timeout logs
gateway/proxy timeout messages

Signs:

“request timed out”
“context cancelled”
“ETIMEDOUT”
🔐 7. Check authentication/authorization logs

Sometimes 500 happens due to misconfigured auth logic.

Look for:

null user/session objects
token parsing errors
expired or invalid JWT handling bugs

Example:

Cannot read property 'role' of null



A request ID and a correlation ID are basically tracking identifiers that help you follow a single request through your entire system (API gateway → services → database → logs).

They’re extremely useful when debugging 500 errors.

🧾 What is a Request ID?

A Request ID is a unique ID assigned to one HTTP request.

Example:

req-7f3a91c2

It helps you answer:

“What happened to THIS exact API call?”

Usually:

generated by the server OR gateway
included in response headers
logged in every service log line
🔗 What is a Correlation ID?

A Correlation ID is a broader tracking ID that follows a request across multiple services.

Example flow:

API Gateway → User Service → Payment Service → DB

All services share the same ID:

corr-91ab22f

It helps answer:

“What happened across the entire system for this request?”

⚖️ Difference (simple)
Type	Scope	Purpose
Request ID	Single service	Track one API call
Correlation ID	Multiple services	Track full request journey
🧠 Why they matter for 500 errors

Without them:

you search logs manually (hard)
you don’t know which request failed

With them:

you search one ID
you see full trace in seconds

Example:

grep corr-91ab22f logs

You instantly see:

API request
DB call
error stack trace
📦 Where do you get Request/Correlation ID?
1. From API response headers (most common)

After calling API in Postman, check:

In Postman:

👉 Go to Response → Headers

Look for:

x-request-id: abc123
x-correlation-id: xyz789

Sometimes names vary:

X-Request-ID
X-Correlation-ID
trace-id
x-amzn-trace-id (AWS)
2. From server logs

Example log:

2026-05-12T10:00:01 req-id=abc123 ERROR NullPointerException

Search using:

grep abc123 app.log
3. From Postman (if you send it manually)

You can also send your own ID:

In Postman:

Go to Headers tab and add:

x-request-id: test-12345
x-correlation-id: test-12345

Then backend should:

pick it up
reuse it in logs
4. If backend auto-generates it

Most frameworks generate it automatically:

Node.js (Express)
middleware like express-request-id
Spring Boot (Java)
filters / Sleuth tracing
Nginx
can inject:
proxy_set_header X-Request-ID $request_id;
🧪 How to check if your API supports it (Postman test)
Step 1: Send request in Postman
Step 2: Open Response Headers
Step 3: Look for:
x-request-id
x-correlation-id

If you see it → system supports tracing.

If not → it’s not implemented.

🛠️ How developers implement it (simple idea)
Middleware example logic:
Check if request has ID
If not → generate one
Attach it to logs + response headers

Pseudo:

req.id = req.headers['x-request-id'] || generateUUID()
res.setHeader('x-request-id', req.id)
🔍 How you use it for debugging 500 errors

When API fails:

Step 1:

Copy request ID from Postman response

Step 2:

Search logs:

grep x-request-id=abc123 logs
Step 3:

You’ll see:

exact error
service that failed
stack trace

Common causes of 503:
App is down / crashed
Too many requests (overloaded workers)
Uvicorn not running
NGINX cannot reach backend
Health check failing


A correlation ID is a unique identifier used to track a single request as it moves through multiple systems or services.

It’s basically a “tracking number” for a request in distributed systems.

🟦 Simple definition

A correlation ID = a unique ID attached to one request so you can follow it across logs and services

🧠 Why it is needed

In modern systems (microservices), one user request may pass through:

Client → API Gateway → Service A → Service B → Database

Without a correlation ID:

logs are scattered
hard to find where failure happened

With correlation ID:

all logs can be grouped together
you can trace full journey of one request
📦 Example

Request comes in:

Correlation-ID: abc123

Now every service logs it:

API Gateway log:
[abc123] Request received
Service A log:
[abc123] Fetching user data
Database log:
[abc123] Query executed
Error log:
[abc123] NullPointerException in service A

What is happening during testing?

When you hit an API in FastAPI:

Request goes in
System generates (or receives) a correlation ID
Same ID is added to:
response headers
server logs
downstream service logs
🧠 Where you SEE the correlation ID
✅ 1. In API response (most common)

Example response header:

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
X-Correlation-ID: 7f3a9c21-abc123

👉 This is the easiest way to find it during testing.

✅ 2. In server logs

Example:

INFO [7f3a9c21] Request started
ERROR [7f3a9c21] Database failure
✅ 3. In API Gateway / proxy logs

If using NGINX:

[7f3a9c21] GET /api/user 500
✅ 4. In testing tools
Postman / curl
curl:
curl -i http://localhost:8000/api/user

Look in response headers:

X-Correlation-ID: abc123
Postman:
Go to Headers tab
Check response headers
Find X-Correlation-ID


Debugging a failing API using logs is basically a systematic way of reconstructing what happened to a request—especially when you see errors like 500 or 503.

Here’s a practical, real-world approach.

🧠 1. Start with the error + context

First you need:

API endpoint (e.g. /users/login)
Time of failure
Request details (payload, headers if possible)
Status code (500 / 503 / etc.)

👉 Without this, logs are hard to search.

🟦 2. Get the correlation/request ID (MOST IMPORTANT)

If your system uses a correlation ID:

Example response/header:

X-Correlation-ID: 7f3a9c21

This is your main debugging key.

🟩 3. Search logs using that ID
In application logs (FastAPI / backend logs):
grep 7f3a9c21 app.log

In FastAPI logs, you’ll typically see:

INFO [7f3a9c21] request started
ERROR [7f3a9c21] NullPointerException at user_service.py:45

👉 This tells you EXACTLY where it failed.

🟧 4. Check server logs (if needed)

If it’s a 503 / gateway issue, check infrastructure logs:

In NGINX:

cat /var/log/nginx/error.log | grep 7f3a9c21

You may see:

upstream timed out (110: Connection timed out)
🟥 5. Identify where failure happened

Now classify the error:

🔴 Application failure (500)

Look for:

stack trace
null pointer
DB query error

Example:

TypeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'id'

👉 Problem is in your code or logic

🔴 Infrastructure failure (503)

Look for:

timeout
connection refused
service down

Example:

upstream service unavailable

👉 Problem is server, load balancer, or dependency

🟨 6. Trace the request flow

Follow logs step-by-step:

Client → API Gateway → Service → Database

Check logs in order:

Gateway log (did request arrive?)
App log (did it execute?)
DB log (did query run?)


501 – Not Implemented is an HTTP status code that means:

👉 The server does not support the functionality required to fulfill the request.

🟦 Simple meaning

The client asked the server to do something, but:

“I don’t know how to do this request type (or feature is not built yet).”

🧠 Easy example

Imagine you call an API:

POST /payments/crypto

But the backend only supports:

credit card payments
UPI payments

👉 So server replies:

501 Not Implemented
🟩 When does 501 happen?
1. Feature not built yet
API endpoint exists in docs but not implemented in code
2. Unsupported HTTP method

Example:

TRACE /api/user

Server only supports GET/POST → returns 501

3. Missing backend capability
Server cannot process requested protocol or feature
4. Placeholder or stub server
Developers created route but didn’t implement logic yet
🟨 Example in real APIs

If you use FastAPI:

@app.get("/new-feature")
def new_feature():
    raise NotImplementedError()

👉 This can lead to:

501 Not Implemented
⚖️ 501 vs other errors
Code	Meaning
400	Bad request (your input is wrong)
401	Unauthorized (login needed)
404	Not found
500	Server crashed (bug/error)
501	Feature not supported at all
🧠 Simple analogy

Think of a restaurant:

You order “intergalactic pizza”

Restaurant says:

“We don’t make that dish at all”

👉 That’s 501

🔥 Key takeaway

501 = Server is working, but the feature you asked for is not implemented or supported.


502 – Bad Gateway means the server you’re talking to (usually a proxy or load balancer) got an invalid response from another server upstream.

It’s not usually your client’s fault. It’s a communication problem between servers.

🟦 Simple meaning

👉 “I (gateway/load balancer) tried to get response from backend server, but it failed or returned something invalid.”

🧠 Where it happens

Typically in this flow:

Client → NGINX / Load Balancer → Backend API (FastAPI, etc.)

If backend fails or doesn’t respond properly:
👉 502 Bad Gateway

🧩 Common reasons for 502
🔴 1. Backend server is down
Your API server is not running
Process crashed

Example:

FastAPI stopped
NGINX can’t reach it → 502
🔴 2. Wrong upstream configuration
NGINX pointing to wrong IP/port

Example:

proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:9000;

But app is running on port 8000 → failure

🔴 3. Timeout from backend
Backend takes too long to respond
Gateway gives up waiting
🔴 4. Crashed application / runtime error
Memory crash
Unhandled exception causing process exit
🔴 5. Network issues between services
Firewall blocking connection
DNS issues inside cluster
🔴 6. Bad response from backend
Backend sends corrupted/invalid HTTP response
Gateway cannot understand it
🔴 7. Overloaded server
Too many requests
Backend cannot handle traffic


504 – Gateway Timeout means:

👉 A gateway or proxy server (like NGINX or a load balancer) waited too long for a response from the backend server, but didn’t get it in time.

So it gives up and returns 504.

🟦 Simple meaning

“I asked the backend for a response, but it took too long, so I timed out.”

🧠 Where it happens

Typical flow:

Client → Gateway / Load Balancer → Backend API (FastAPI, DB, etc.)

If backend is slow or stuck:
👉 504 Gateway Timeout

🧩 Common reasons for 504
🔴 1. Slow backend processing
API takes too long to respond
Heavy computation (ML, reports, big queries)

Example in FastAPI:

long database query
infinite loop
slow external API call
🔴 2. Database delay
Slow SQL queries
Missing indexes
Locked tables

👉 Backend is waiting for DB → gateway times out

🔴 3. External service delay
Payment gateway slow
Third-party API not responding
🔴 4. Server overload
Too many requests
CPU/memory maxed out
Workers blocked
🔴 5. Wrong timeout configuration

In NGINX:

default timeout too low
backend needs more time than allowed

Example config:

proxy_read_timeout 30s;

If backend takes 60s → 504

🔴 6. Network delay / packet loss
unstable connection between services
cloud network issues


1. First confirm it’s a real issue (not a testing artifact)

Before blaming the API:

Run the same request using multiple tools:
Postman
curl
automated script (RestAssured / JMeter / k6)
Check from different networks (office, home, VPN on/off)
Check if delay is consistent or intermittent

👉 If all tools show similar delay → likely server-side issue
👉 If only one tool shows delay → tooling/client issue

2. Break down where time is spent (very important)

A REST call has multiple phases:

DNS lookup
TCP connection
TLS handshake (if HTTPS)
Server processing time
Response transfer time
How to measure:

Use curl timing:

curl -w "@curl-format.txt" -o /dev/null -s "https://api.example.com"

Example curl-format.txt:

namelookup:  %{time_namelookup}\n
connect:     %{time_connect}\n
appconnect:  %{time_appconnect}\n
starttransfer: %{time_starttransfer}\n
total:       %{time_total}\n
What this tells you:
High time_connect → network issue
High time_starttransfer → backend processing issue (MOST IMPORTANT)
High time_total only → response payload/network transfer issue
3. Isolate backend vs network

Try:

Call API from:
local machine
server in same region (AWS EC2 / Azure VM if possible)
Use ping and traceroute

If latency disappears in same region → network latency problem

4. Check server-side logs (this is where real QA work starts)

Ask dev/ops for:

API gateway logs
application logs
request start vs end timestamps

Look for:

slow SQL queries
external API calls
retries/timeouts
thread pool saturation

👉 If logs show request processed in 200ms but client sees 10s → network / gateway issue
👉 If logs show 10s processing → backend problem

5. Common root causes of 10–20 sec API delay
Backend issues:
Slow database queries (missing indexes)
N+1 query problem
Heavy business logic in request thread
Synchronous calls to other services
Deadlocks or locking in DB


What is curl?

curl (Client URL) is a command-line tool used to send HTTP requests to APIs and view responses.

Think of it as:

“Postman in terminal form”

It helps you test:

REST APIs (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE)
response time
headers
payloads
authentication
Where can you run curl in Windows?
Option 1: Windows Command Prompt (CMD)
Press Win + R → type cmd → Enter
Then type:
curl https://api.example.com
Option 2: PowerShell (recommended)
Press Win + X → PowerShell or Windows Terminal
Run:
curl https://api.example.com

⚠️ Note: In PowerShell, curl may sometimes alias to Invoke-WebRequest, so you can use:

curl.exe https://api.example.com
Option 3: Git Bash (best for QA engineers)

If you install Git:

Install Git: https://git-scm.com/
Open Git Bash
Run curl normally:
curl https://api.example.com
Basic curl command
curl https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1

This means:

Send GET request
Get response from API
Print output in terminal
Important curl flags (very important for QA)
1. Show full response timing (MOST USEFUL FOR YOU)
curl -w "@curl-format.txt" -o /dev/null -s https://api.example.com

You create a file curl-format.txt:

namelookup:  %{time_namelookup}\n
connect:     %{time_connect}\n
appconnect:  %{time_appconnect}\n
starttransfer: %{time_starttransfer}\n
total:       %{time_total}\n
What you check:
time_namelookup → DNS issue
time_connect → network issue
time_starttransfer → backend processing time (VERY IMPORTANT)
time_total → full response time
2. GET request (default)
curl https://api.example.com/users
3. POST request with JSON body
curl -X POST https://api.example.com/login ^
-H "Content-Type: application/json" ^
-d "{\"username\":\"test\",\"password\":\"1234\"}"
4. Add headers (auth token)
curl https://api.example.com/data ^
-H "Authorization: Bearer your_token_here"
5. See response headers
curl -I https://api.example.com
6. Save response to file
curl https://api.example.com -o response.json
How QA uses curl in real testing


1. Trace ID vs Request ID vs Correlation ID (simple meaning)
🔹 Trace ID
Used in distributed systems (microservices)
Tracks one request across multiple services
Generated by tracing tools or API gateway

👉 Example:

traceId = a1b2c3d4
🔹 Request ID
Identifies one single API request
Usually generated by:
API gateway OR
backend server

👉 Example:

requestId = 98765
🔹 Correlation ID
Used to link logs across systems
Often same purpose as traceId in many companies
Sometimes manually passed from frontend → backend

👉 Example:

correlationId = xyz-123
2. Where do you get these IDs?

This is the most important QA part.

🔥 A. In API Response (BEST CASE)

Sometimes APIs return it directly:

{
  "status": "success",
  "traceId": "abc123",
  "requestId": "req789"
}

👉 You copy it from Postman or response body.

🔥 B. In Response Headers

Very common in real systems:

X-Request-Id: 12345
X-Correlation-Id: abc-xyz
X-Trace-Id: t-7890
You check this in:
Postman → Headers tab
curl → -i option

Example:

curl -i https://api.example.com
🔥 C. In Server Logs (MOST COMMON IN QA WORK)

Example:

INFO [traceId=abc123] Request received
INFO [traceId=abc123] DB query started

👉 You search logs using these IDs.
